content:
@lazynovice - So you are saying that, in the case of individual stocks, each purchased lot must be handled separately, even if I typically report the single sale as aggregate gain on my tax return.

Do you agree that this is not the case for mutual funds if you are reporting using the average cost method?
If my example changed to mutual fund shares, the average cost would be below the sale price and there would be no wash sale. I am certain about this.

They typically also can't take-away/transfer-away your "status", such a "high-roller" benefits at a casino or two, or by travelling full-priced first-class tickets to achieve lifetime Diamond status at an airline, or some heirloom jewelry (hard to value/find), tuition gifts you may possibly pay for your kith/kin -- just thinking aloud ..

IANL - always stay clear like "Lee_WSP" said above;

Umbrella of about $2 million usually, a plenty; If somone want to crack above that - they might as well sue you for $10 million.. and good-luck for them to collect

@exodusNH: Thanks for the 50% observation. That explains it... This will also mean that since I will be putting close to $18k on the card that gives me 0% APR with $18k max limit, I will be dinging my credit for another year anyway. Good to know.
@tashnewbie: Yes. I have a loan of ~$700k on the new home. I expect the old home to sell at $750k. I plan to put ~$450k from the sale and bring down the mortgage from $700 to ~$250k and then the left over $300k in QQQ or SPY for the coming few years.
There is also a good chance that I might just pay off the new home fully. Don't know exactly which of the two options I will pick once the old home sells... I lived there for all of the last 9 years so there should be no tax; purchase price was $300k and the upgrades should add up to $25k; so up to $825k sell price should be tax-free...
